Data Ingestion Factory + Builder Workflow
This module demonstrates a scalable, extensible design for building multiple types of data sources in a real-time analytics platform using Factory + Builder patterns.
1. Overview
The system supports multiple data sources, including:
Kafka
AWS SQS
RabbitMQ
Custom Webhook feeds
Each data source has:
A connect() method for establishing connection
A fetch() method to retrieve data
The design separates:
Data source configuration
Data source creation
Client usage
2. Design Patterns Used
Builder Pattern
Encapsulates the construction of complex data source objects.
Supports mandatory and optional parameters.
Example: Kafka.KafkaBuilder, AwsSqs.SqsBuilder.
Factory Pattern
DataSourceConfigFactory: Creates configuration objects for each data source type.
DataSourceFactory: Creates concrete Datasource instances using configuration objects and builders.
3. Workflow
Client selects a data source type (e.g., "KAFKA").
DataSourceConfigFactory builds the appropriate configuration object (KafkaConfig, AwsSqsConfig, etc.).
DataSourceFactory uses the configuration object to:
Pick the right Builder for the data source
Construct the concrete Datasource object
Client calls connect() and fetch() on the datasource.


Example Client Usage:

    DataSourceConfig config = DataSourceConfigFactory.createConfig("KAFKA");
    Datasource kafka = DataSourceFactory.getDataSource(config);

    kafka.connect();
    kafka.fetch();

Optional runtime switch: Client can change data sources at runtime without modifying code. 

    DataSourceConfig sqsConfig = DataSourceConfigFactory.createConfig("SQS");
    Datasource sqs = DataSourceFactory.getDataSource(sqsConfig);
    sqs.connect();
    sqs.fetch();


ADVANTAGES :

    Open/Closed Principle: Adding new data sources requires minimal code changes.
    Separation of Concerns: Config creation, object construction, and usage are decoupled.
    Readability & Maintainability: Fluent builders make object creation clean and intuitive.
    Production-ready: Can be extended to read configs from JSON, YAML, or environment variables.
    Scalable: Works for complex systems with multiple data sources and varying initialization parameters.